WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
Many serialization defects involve lengths, counts, nesting, and allocation.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:16.120
A length field may be wrong, unexpectedly large, negative after conversion, inconsistent with the remaining input, or inconsistent with related fields.

3
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:19.980
A count field may drive loops or allocations.

4
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.600
Nested data can consume stack, heap, or CPU if depth and complexity are not limited.

5
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.640
Defensive parsers validate sizes before allocation.

6
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:38.220
They check that the declared size fits within the remaining input, within the maximum allowed message size, and within the application's memory policy.

7
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.360
They also check arithmetic carefully.

8
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:48.340
Size calculations can overflow before allocation, especially when counts, element sizes, and offsets are combined.

9
00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.740
Availability is part of secure parsing.

10
00:00:51.840 --> 00:01:03.760
A parser that accepts unlimited nesting, unlimited repeated fields, unlimited string lengths, or unlimited queued records can be correct for small examples and unsafe under pressure.

11
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:14.180
Limits should cover message size, individual field size, collection counts, nesting depth, parse time, and retained memory where those controls apply.

12
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.540
Failure behavior should be explicit.

13
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:23.360
When limits are exceeded, the parser should reject the input, release temporary resources, and report a safe error.

14
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:29.060
The program should not continue with truncated data, partially constructed objects, or guessed defaults.

15
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.880
Resource limits are not just performance settings; they are part of the format contract.

16
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:40.480
Limits should also be monitored where the parser is exposed to production traffic or large data sets.

17
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:48.740
Frequent limit failures can signal bad clients, incompatible data, or sizing assumptions that need product review, and the response should be predictable.
